	<entry>
		<id>https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/a-e-w-mason/the-house-of-the-arrow</id>
		<dc:identifier>https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/a-e-w-mason/the-house-of-the-arrow</dc:identifier>
		<title>The House of the Arrow</title>
		<author>
			<name>A. E. W. Mason</name>
			<uri>https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/a-e-w-mason</uri>
			<schema:alternateName>Alfred Edward Woodley Mason</schema:alternateName>
			<schema:sameAs>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A._E._W._Mason</schema:sameAs>
			<schema:sameAs>https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n50043662.html</schema:sameAs>
		</author>
		<published>2026-10-01T21:03:08Z</published>
		<dc:issued>2026-10-01T21:03:08Z</dc:issued>
		<updated>2026-10-01T21:03:41Z</updated>
		<dc:language>en-GB</dc:language>
		<dc:publisher>Standard Ebooks</dc:publisher>
		<rights>Public domain in the United States. Users located outside of the United States must check their local laws before using this ebook. Original content released to the public domain via the Creative Commons CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication.</rights>
		<summary type="text">A blackmailer falsely accuses a dead woman’s niece of murder by poison, but the evidence suggests the accusation might not be false after all.</summary>
		<content type="html">&lt;p&gt;After the apparently natural death of wealthy widow Madame Harlowe, a blackmailer accuses her niece of murder by poison—an accusation he knows to be false. The famous French detective &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/collections/inspector-hanaud"&gt;Inspector Hanaud&lt;/a&gt; is called in, with Madame Harlowe’s death only an excuse to investigate a series of anonymous blackmailings the local police have been helpless to solve.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The police exhume Madame Harlowe’s body and find no trace of poison, defeating the blackmailer—yet there’s a treatise on untraceable poison in the house library. Perhaps the accusation was really true?&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/a-e-w-mason"&gt;A. E. W. Mason&lt;/a&gt; had learned about untraceable poisons during his time as a Secret Service agent in 1915.&lt;/p&gt;</content>
		<category scheme="http://purl.org/dc/terms/LCSH" term="Detective and mystery stories"/>
		<category scheme="http://purl.org/dc/terms/LCSH" term="Fiction"/>
		<category scheme="http://purl.org/dc/terms/LCSH" term="Inheritance and succession--Fiction"/>
		<category scheme="http://purl.org/dc/terms/LCSH" term="Murder--Investigation--Fiction"/>
		<category scheme="https://standardebooks.org/vocab/subjects" term="Fiction"/>
		<category scheme="https://standardebooks.org/vocab/subjects" term="Mystery"/>
		<link href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/a-e-w-mason/the-house-of-the-arrow/downloads/cover.jpg" rel="http://opds-spec.org/image" type="image/jpeg"/>
