	</entry>
	<entry>
		<id>https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/thomas-mann/short-fiction/various-translators</id>
		<dc:identifier>https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/thomas-mann/short-fiction/various-translators</dc:identifier>
		<title>Short Fiction</title>
		<author>
			<name>Thomas Mann</name>
			<uri>https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/thomas-mann</uri>
			<schema:alternateName>Paul Thomas Mann</schema:alternateName>
			<schema:sameAs>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Mann</schema:sameAs>
			<schema:sameAs>https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n78095625.html</schema:sameAs>
		</author>
		<published>2026-09-09T16:48:38Z</published>
		<dc:issued>2026-09-09T16:48:38Z</dc:issued>
		<updated>2026-09-22T13:48:52Z</updated>
		<dc:language>en-GB</dc:language>
		<dc:publisher>Standard Ebooks</dc:publisher>
		<rights>Public domain in the United States. Users located outside of the United States must check their local laws before using this ebook. Original content released to the public domain via the Creative Commons CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication.</rights>
		<summary type="text">A collection of Thomas Mann’s short stories, ordered by date of their original publication.</summary>
		<content type="html">&lt;p&gt;By the time &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/thomas-mann"&gt;Thomas Mann’s&lt;/a&gt; longer work &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/thomas-mann/buddenbrooks/h-t-lowe-porter"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Buddenbrooks&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; finally established his fame, he had already been a published author of short fiction, primarily in the satirical magazine &lt;i&gt;Simplicissimus&lt;/i&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;A typical Mann short story always has some element of psychological disquiet. Earlier in his career this often manifested in his stories as behaviors that caused the protagonists to endure ridicule (“Little Louise,” “The Path to the Cemetery”), but this evolved into entire characters who combine extra-societal behavior with a seeming inability to halt their descent into chaos (“Death in Venice,” “Mario and the Magician”). Woven through this are common themes: the secret world of children, the social mores of Wilhelmine Germany, and a love of dogs.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;This collection includes many of Mann’s best known works. Particularly famous is “Death in Venice,” the candidly autobiographical story of a staid author’s obsession with a beautiful boy, which the &lt;i&gt;Encyclopædia Britannica&lt;/i&gt; included in its &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/collections/encyclopaedia-britannicas-great-books-of-the-western-world"&gt;Great Books of the Western World&lt;/a&gt; set.&lt;/p&gt;</content>
		<category scheme="http://purl.org/dc/terms/LCSH" term="Psychological fiction, German"/>
		<category scheme="http://purl.org/dc/terms/LCSH" term="Middle-aged men--Fiction"/>
		<category scheme="https://standardebooks.org/vocab/subjects" term="Fiction"/>
		<category scheme="https://standardebooks.org/vocab/subjects" term="Shorts"/>
		<link href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/thomas-mann/short-fiction/various-translators/downloads/cover.jpg" rel="http://opds-spec.org/image" type="image/jpeg"/>
		<link href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/thomas-mann/short-fiction/various-translators/downloads/cover-thumbnail.jpg" rel="http://opds-spec.org/image/thumbnail" type="image/jpeg"/>
